@startuml "Bridge - Window (Problem) - Class Diagram"
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
title Before — the platform is a superclass (the problem, GoF p. 151)


abstract class Window {
  + drawContents() : void
  + drawBorder() : void
  + {abstract} drawRect(..) : void
  + {abstract} drawText(..) : void
}

class XWindow {
  + drawRect(..) : void
  + drawText(..) : void
}
class PMWindow {
  + drawRect(..) : void
  + drawText(..) : void
}

abstract class IconWindow {
  + drawContents() : void
}
class XIconWindow {
  + drawRect(..) : void
  + drawText(..) : void
}
class PMIconWindow {
  + drawRect(..) : void
  + drawText(..) : void
}

abstract class TransientWindow {
  + drawContents() : void
}
class XTransientWindow {
  + drawRect(..) : void
  + drawText(..) : void
}
class PMTransientWindow {
  + drawRect(..) : void
  + drawText(..) : void
}

Window <|-- XWindow
Window <|-- PMWindow
Window <|-- IconWindow
Window <|-- TransientWindow
IconWindow <|-- XIconWindow
IconWindow <|-- PMIconWindow
TransientWindow <|-- XTransientWindow
TransientWindow <|-- PMTransientWindow

note right of XIconWindow
  Inherits the <i>icon</i> behavior,
  and must <b>copy</b> the X drawing
  code out of XWindow. A class
  cannot inherit from both.
end note

note as N1
  <b>One hierarchy, two reasons to subclass.</b>
  "Icon window" is a kind of window. "X window" is
  also a kind of window. The hierarchy can only
  express one of them, so the leaves are the
  product of the two:

     3 window kinds  x  2 platforms  =  6 classes
     a 3rd platform  ->  9        a 4th kind -> 8

  And GoF's second objection (p. 151): the client
  writes `new XIconWindow(..)`, so <b>client code
  becomes platform-dependent</b> — the platform is
  chosen when the code is compiled, not when the
  program runs.
end note

@enduml
